        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mau Mau rebellion</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">The British Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>: the Kikuyu districts of Central Province, the Aberdares and the forests around Mount Kenya, Nairobi, the Rift Valley and Meru.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Oathing from about 1943; the State of Emergency declared on 20 October 1952; Operation Anvil (1954); the capture of Dedan Kimathi on 21 October 1956; fighting by Meru units until after independence; an amnesty and army action in 1964 and 1965.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Field Marshal Dedan Kimathi; General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; Field Marshal Musa Mwariama; General Baimungi; Field Marshal Muthoni; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a>, tried as one of the Kapenguria Six; on the British side Governor Evelyn Baring and General George Erskine.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The British campaign ended with Kimathi's capture in 1956 after mass detention, villagisation and 1,090 executions; at least 11,000 deaths among the Mau Mau and other forces, with some estimates considerably higher; Kenyan majority rule was set in motion in January 1960, and independence came in December 1963.</span></div>
