    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> Land and Freedom Army, called the Mau Mau, rose against British colonial rule in Kenya from 1952, after decades of land seizure, forced wage labour and failed petitions; Wangari Maathai names its three issues as land, freedom and self-governance.</li><li>The British answered with a State of Emergency, Operation Anvil, a detention system called the Pipeline and the forced resettlement of more than a million Kikuyu in fortified villages; 1,090 people were hanged.</li><li>Historians still dispute the death toll, with estimates of 25,000 and 50,000 and a claim of up to 300,000, and whether the conflict was a nationalist rising, an intra-Kikuyu civil war, or both.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>By the end of the Emergency, 1,090 Mau Mau suspects had been executed by hanging, more than double the number executed by the French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, and at no other time or place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> was capital punishment dispensed so aggressively.</p></aside>
    </section>

